3. Biological State Space

3.1 Compositional Microbiome Data: Corrected Zero Handling

Skin microbiome data are compositional. The Aitchison distance on CLR-transformed compositions is mathematically appropriate but not universally superior to Bray–Curtis or UniFrac; sensitivity analysis across metrics is mandated.

Zero handling is specified on counts, not relative abundances. For count matrix X = (x_{ij}), the Bayesian-multiplicative replacement (cmultRepl; zCompositions R package) is applied:

x_{ij}^{\text{repl}} =
\begin{cases}
x_{ij}, & x_{ij} > 0 \\
\hat{\delta}_j, & x_{ij} = 0
\end{cases}

where \hat{\delta}_j is the posterior mean of the zero cell under a Dirichlet prior with geometric mean marginals. The replacement preserves the compositional total in expectation, avoiding the geometric distortion introduced by substituting relative abundances and re-closing. CLR is then applied to the replaced counts after closure.

3.2 Absolute Abundance: PMA and Quantitative Microbiome Profiling

CLR ignores total microbial load. Propidium monoazide (PMA) treatment combined with droplet digital PCR (ddPCR) discriminates DNA from viable cells and overcomes relic-DNA bias. Thomas et al. (2025, Environmental Microbiome) demonstrated that PMA at 2.5–15 µM reduced 16S rRNA gene copies by 24–44% relative to untreated samples, and that quantitative microbiome profiling (QMP) captured significant shifts in community composition that relative microbiome profiling (RMP) failed to detect at the ASV level.

Absolute abundance is therefore a separate vector from compositional metrics:

\text{Abund}(t) = \left(\text{qPCR}, \; \text{CFU}, \; \text{PMA-ddPCR}\right)

3.3 Functional Profiling and Network Topology

Compositional distance alone may miss functional disruption. Cheviti et al. (2025, Br J Dermatol 193(Suppl_2):ii40) demonstrated that daily use of mild cleansers — glycerine-containing cast melt bars for 4 weeks, and bodywashes containing plant-derived submicron occlusives for 2 weeks — strengthened microbiome co-occurrence network properties without significantly affecting diversity. The increase in microbial co-occurrence network indicates resilience to regular use of such formulations.

The functional profile is defined as

\Phi(\mathbf{M}) = \left( d_{\text{microbe}},\; \text{network connectivity},\; \text{modularity},\; \text{functional gene abundance} \right)

3.4 Recovery Time

Recovery is defined by a pre-registered threshold:

\tau_{\text{rec}}(\epsilon) = \inf\{ t : d_{\text{microbe}}(\mathbf{M}(t), \mathbf{M}(0)) \le \epsilon \}

with defaults \epsilon_{\text{default}} = 1 SD of baseline Aitchison distance (primary), \epsilon_{\text{default, BC}} = 0.3 Bray–Curtis dissimilarity (secondary), and \tau_{\text{rec}} = \infty if no recovery within T = 28 days. Deviations require pre-registration.

TEWL recovery kinetics provide an empirical reference: after SLS patch removal, TEWL recovery is 51.55 \pm 24.73\% at 24 h and 76.61 \pm 11.14\% at four days, with irritation persisting 7–9 days (perturbation-and-recovery study, 2000). This anchors the observation window for barrier parameters.

