4. Formal Framework

4.1 Parameter and Admissible Space

The parameter space is

u = (\theta_{\text{formulation}},\; \theta_{\text{process}},\; \theta_{\text{use}})

The admissible space is the union of three enumerated grids:

\mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}} = \mathcal{U}_{\text{conv}} \;\cup\; \mathcal{M}_{\text{BCME}} \;\cup\; \mathcal{A}_{\text{add}}

Conventional grid. \mathcal{U}_{\text{conv}} = \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{P}, where \mathcal{S} contains 12 surfactant families (SLS, LAS, SLES, APG, FAE, PGE-OE, AO, amphoacetate, betaine, sulfosuccinate, glucoside, isethionate), \mathcal{C} = \{0.5\%, 1.5\%, 3.0\%\}, and \mathcal{P} = \{4.5, 5.0, 5.5, 6.0, 7.0\}. Hence |\mathcal{U}_{\text{conv}}| = 180.

BCME subgrid. \mathcal{M}_{\text{BCME}} = \mathcal{O} \times \mathcal{K} \times \mathcal{W} \times \mathcal{N}, where \mathcal{O} contains 3 oil phases (oleic acid, squalane, triolein), \mathcal{K} contains 3 cosurfactants (ethanol, propanol, isopropanol), \mathcal{W} = \{60\%, 70\%, 80\%\}, and \mathcal{N} = \{0\%, 0.5\%\} sodium surfactin. Hence |\mathcal{M}_{\text{BCME}}| = 54.

Additive grid. \mathcal{A}_{\text{add}} = \mathcal{P}_{\text{poly}} \times \mathcal{L}_{\text{lipid}} \times \mathcal{C}_{\text{add}}, where \mathcal{P}_{\text{poly}} contains 3 polymers (HPMC, xanthan gum, PEG-150 distearate), \mathcal{L}_{\text{lipid}} contains 3 lipids (ceramide NP, cholesterol, free fatty acid mix), and \mathcal{C}_{\text{add}} = \{0.5\%, 1.5\%\}. The additive grid is applied as a one-factor refinement to the top-K non-dominated base formulations, with K = 20 pre-registered. Hence the additive contribution to the confirmatory space is at most 20 \times 18 = 360 refinements, of which only those passing regulatory filtering enter.

The total admissible cardinality is bounded:

|\mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}| \le 180 + 54 + 360 = 594

of which at most 180 are base formulations. This is the sense in which the space is bounded: every element is enumerable before data collection, and no unenumerated second-stage expansion is permitted.

4.2 Regulatory Filter

Regulatory constraints are applied as a post-hoc filter, not a design factor:

\mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}' = \{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}} \mid u \text{ satisfies } \mathcal{R}\}

The constraint set \mathcal{R} includes, but is not limited to, EU Regulation 1223/2009 as amended by Commission Regulation (EU) 2017/1224, which restricts methylisothiazolinone to a maximum of 15 ppm (0.0015% w/w) in rinse-off cosmetic products. Because \mathcal{R} is a constraint rather than a Cartesian factor, it does not enter the cardinality |\mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}| multiplicatively.

4.3 State and Perturbation Vectors

The biological state vector is

\mathbf{X}(t) =
\begin{bmatrix}
\text{pH}(t) \\
\text{TEWL}(t) \\
H(t) \\
L(t) \\
P(t) \\
\mathbf{M}(t) \\
\text{Abund}(t)
\end{bmatrix}

The expanded perturbation vector is

\mathcal{D}_{\text{bio}}(u) =
\left(
\Delta\text{pH},\,
\Delta\text{TEWL},\,
\Delta L,\,
\Delta P,\,
P_{\text{cum}},\,
D_{\max},\,
\tau_{\text{rec}},\,
I_{\text{irr}},\,
\Delta \text{Abund}
\right)

4.4 Efficacy Functional and Non-Inferiority

Let R(u) denote the detersion efficacy functional, measured by gravimetric sebum removal under standardized conditions. The efficacy constraint is defined by non-inferiority against baseline:

R(u) \ge R(u_{\text{base}}) - \Delta_{\text{NI}}

with \Delta_{\text{NI}} pre-registered as 10\% of baseline sebum removal. This replaces the ambiguous absolute threshold of earlier formulations. The non-inferiority margin is a decision-theoretic parameter, not a statistical one, and is fixed prior to data collection.

4.5 Primary Subspace: Decision-Theoretic Derivation

Strict dominance on all nine dimensions is too strong: measurement noise on any single dimension defeats dominance. The primary subspace is derived from a decision-theoretic loss rather than measurement convenience.

Let \mathcal{L}(u) = \sum_{i=1}^{9} w_i\, \phi_i\!\left(\mathcal{D}_{\text{bio},i}(u)\right) be the loss functional, with w_i \ge 0 the decision weights and \phi_i monotone increasing. The primary subspace is the set of dimensions satisfying two conditions simultaneously:

\mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}} = \left\{ i : w_i \frac{\partial \phi_i}{\partial \mathcal{D}_i} \text{ is non-negligible} \;\land\; \text{Var}(\hat{\mathcal{D}}_i) \text{ is bounded} \right\}

The first condition selects dimensions that materially affect the decision; the second excludes dimensions whose measurement variance renders dominance untestable. Applying this criterion yields

\mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}} = \left( \Delta\text{pH},\; \Delta\text{TEWL},\; D_{\max},\; \tau_{\text{rec}},\; \Delta\text{Abund} \right)

Recovery time \tau_{\text{rec}} is in the primary subspace. The earlier relegation of \tau_{\text{rec}} to exploratory status was backwards on decision grounds: a formulation with small acute \Delta\text{TEWL} but \tau_{\text{rec}} = \infty is strictly worse than one with a larger initial perturbation and fast recovery. Recovery is the decision-relevant quantity for cumulative skin health. The remaining dimensions \{\Delta L, \Delta P, P_{\text{cum}}, I_{\text{irr}}\} are exploratory; P_{\text{cum}} is a derived quantity of \tau_{\text{rec}} and D_{\max} and is not independently primary.

4.6 \epsilon-Dominance: Formal Definition and Non-Transitivity

For u, v \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}', and with the minimization convention that lower is better on every primary dimension, u \epsilon-dominates v, written u \prec_{\epsilon} v, if and only if

(1 + \epsilon_i)\, \mathcal{D}_{\text{primary},i}(u) \;\le\; \mathcal{D}_{\text{primary},i}(v) \qquad \forall\, i \in \{1, \dots, m\}

with strict inequality for at least one i, where m = 5. This is the multiplicative box-based \epsilon-dominance of Laumanns et al. (2002, Evolutionary Computation 10(3):263–282).

Two formal properties matter.

Non-transitivity. \epsilon-dominance is not transitive in general. If u \prec_{\epsilon} v and v \prec_{\epsilon} w, then (1+\epsilon)^2 \mathcal{D}(u) \le \mathcal{D}(w), which does not imply (1+\epsilon)\mathcal{D}(u) \le \mathcal{D}(w). Consequently \prec_{\epsilon} is not a strict partial order, and the \epsilon-Pareto front must be constructed as a maximal set under \prec_{\epsilon} rather than as a set of optima of a scalar order. This is a known property of box-based \epsilon-dominance and is accommodated by reporting the full maximal set.

Tolerance calibration. The tolerances are derived from instrument reproducibility rather than asserted. For TEWL, the Tewameter TM300 has a documented coefficient of variation on repeated measures; \epsilon_{\text{TEWL}} is set to the upper 95% confidence bound on the relative CV, giving \epsilon_{\text{TEWL}} = 1.5 / \overline{\text{TEWL}}_{\text{base}}. For pH, the glass-electrode reproducibility yields \epsilon_{\text{pH}} = 0.2 / \overline{\text{pH}}_{\text{base}}. Analogous relative tolerances are derived for D_{\max}, \tau_{\text{rec}}, and \Delta\text{Abund} from their respective instrument and assay reproducibilities. No tolerance is asserted without a reproducibility source.

4.7 Central Conjecture

\boxed{
\exists\, u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}' \;:\;
R(u) \ge R(u_{\text{base}}) - \Delta_{\text{NI}}
\;\land\;
\mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u) \prec_{\epsilon} \mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u_{\text{base}})
}

The conjecture asserts the existence of an admissible formulation that is non-inferior in detersion and \epsilon-dominant over baseline on the primary perturbation subspace.

4.8 Local and Global Falsifiability

H_{\text{local}}: \exists\, u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{tested}} \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}' \;:\;
R(u) \ge R(u_{\text{base}}) - \Delta_{\text{NI}}
\;\land\;
\mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u) \prec_{\epsilon} \mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u_{\text{base}})

H_{\text{global}}: \exists\, u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}' \;:\;
R(u) \ge R(u_{\text{base}}) - \Delta_{\text{NI}}
\;\land\;
\mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u) \prec_{\epsilon} \mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u_{\text{base}})

A negative result in \mathcal{U}_{\text{tested}} constrains but does not refute H_{\text{global}}. Exhaustion of \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}' — under the surrogate hierarchy of §4.9 with declared sensitivity and specificity — refutes H_{\text{global}}.

4.9 Surrogate Hierarchy as Sequential Decision Instrument

Exhaustive human-subject testing of all admissible formulations is infeasible. The hierarchy

\text{in vitro} \;\rightarrow\; \text{ex vivo} \;\rightarrow\; \text{in vivo}

is formalized as a sequential screening instrument with pre-registered gates.

Tier 1 (in vitro). CMC, micelle radius, zeta potential, ZN, CD/FTIR. n = 3 independent replicates. Gate: ZN < 200 mg N·100 g⁻¹.

Tier 2 (ex vivo). Human skin explants, TEWL, lipid extraction. n = 6 explants per formulation. Gate: \Delta\text{TEWL} \le 1.5 g/m²/h versus baseline.

Tier 3 (in vivo screening). n = 8 subjects per formulation. Primary endpoint: \Delta\text{TEWL} at 24 h. This tier is a triage stage, not a hypothesis test. The n = 8 sample is a non-inferential effect-size filter; no p-values are reported from Tier 3, and no claim of statistical significance is made.

Tier 4 (in vivo confirmatory). n \ge 32 subjects per non-dominated candidate. Full \epsilon-dominance test and R(u) \ge R(u_{\text{base}}) - \Delta_{\text{NI}}.

The surrogate hierarchy renders H_{\text{global}} falsifiable under the declared screening sensitivities: if no formulation passes all four tiers, H_{\text{global}} is refuted within the sensitivity of the instrument.

4.10 Multiplicity Control

The confirmatory family consists of the Tier 4 tests across the non-dominated candidate set. Multiplicity is controlled using the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure at false discovery rate q = 0.05 across the family. The family is pre-registered, and the procedure is applied once at the confirmatory stage. Tier 3 screening is exempt because it produces no inferential claims.

4.11 Power and Triage: A Strict Distinction

For the confirmatory stage, sample size per formulation is estimated via simulation on the 5-dimensional primary subspace. Assuming effect size d = 0.8 on at least two primary dimensions, \alpha = 0.05, power = 0.80, and 20% dropout, n \ge 32 subjects per formulation. Published estimates for skin microbiome studies support n = 32 for alpha-diversity changes at power 0.8.

The Tier 3 sample n = 8 is not a power-justified sample for any hypothesis. It is a triage threshold, and its operating characteristics (sensitivity, specificity) relative to Tier 4 are pre-registered and reported. The distinction between triage and inference is maintained throughout.

4.12 Baseline Set

u_{\text{base}} \in \{ \text{alkaline soap},\; \text{Dove Sensitive Skin bar},\; \text{CeraVe Hydrating Cleanser},\; u_{\text{ref}} \}

The first three are commercial baselines, pre-registered with specific lot numbers, storage conditions, and blinding. The fourth is a standardized reference: a fixed-composition SLS solution at a specified concentration and pH. The standardized reference ensures cross-study reproducibility, because commercial formulations are proprietary and may vary by region and batch. All baselines are measured under identical conditions.

4.13 Acute versus Cumulative Perturbation

The conjecture is tested separately for acute (single-use, T = 24 h) and cumulative (4-week daily use, T = 28 days) regimes. A formulation may dominate acutely but not cumulatively. H_{\text{local, acute}} is tested first; H_{\text{local, cumulative}} is tested second. The cumulative regime weights \tau_{\text{rec}} and P_{\text{cum}} more heavily, consistent with their decision-theoretic status.

