6. Identifiability, Limitations, and the Representation Gap

Three limitations are structural and are stated explicitly.

Non-injectivity of the perturbation map. The map u \mapsto \mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u) is not injective. Distinct formulations may produce indistinguishable perturbation profiles. The framework therefore does not claim uniqueness of any dominant formulation; it claims existence within the enumerated space.

Non-transitivity of \epsilon-dominance. As established in §4.6, \prec_{\epsilon} is not a strict partial order. The \epsilon-Pareto front is reported as a maximal set, and any scalarization for ranking must be pre-registered separately. This is a property of the relation, not a defect of the framework.

Representation gap between BCME proof-of-concept and the micellar grid. The BCME mechanism of Ito et al. operates through spontaneous emulsification and interfacial tension dynamics, with sodium surfactin as a specific lipopeptide booster. The conventional grid of §4.1 operates through monomer–micelle partitioning and contains no lipopeptide. The BCME subgrid of §4.1 partially bridges this gap by including sodium surfactin as a co-additive and by including oleic acid and squalane as oil phases, but the microemulsion physics is not fully represented by the grid coordinates. The proof-of-concept and the conventional search space are therefore connected analogically, not compositionally. This is a limitation on the transferability of the BCME result to the conventional grid, and it is the primary reason the BCME subgrid is treated as a separate component of \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}} rather than as an extension of \mathcal{U}_{\text{conv}}.

A fourth limitation is scope: the framework tests existence, not optimality. A negative result refutes H_{\text{global}} within the enumerated space; it does not refute the existence of a dominant formulation outside \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}'.

---

7. Conclusion

SSC v1.0 is a falsifiable research program, not a product specification. Its central conjecture is

\boxed{
\exists\, u \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{adm}}' \;:\;
R(u) \ge R(u_{\text{base}}) - \Delta_{\text{NI}}
\;\land\;
\mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u) \prec_{\epsilon} \mathcal{D}_{\text{primary}}(u_{\text{base}})
}

The framework addresses the principal limitations of prior versions. The admissible space is bounded and fully enumerable, with the conventional grid, the BCME subgrid, and a one-factor additive refinement of the top-K base formulations. Regulatory constraints are applied as a filter, not a design factor. The dominance relation is the multiplicative box-based \epsilon-dominance of Laumanns et al., with tolerances derived from instrument reproducibility and with the non-transitivity of the relation explicitly accommodated. The primary subspace is derived from a decision-theoretic loss and includes recovery time as a primary dimension. The global conjecture is rendered falsifiable through a pre-registered surrogate hierarchy with formal screening gates and a strict triage–inference distinction. Multiplicity is controlled by Benjamini–Hochberg at q = 0.05 across the confirmatory family. Compositional zero handling is corrected to count-based Bayesian-multiplicative replacement. The BCME proof-of-concept is anchored to its specific mechanism and to sodium surfactin as the selectivity booster, and the representation gap between that mechanism and the conventional grid is stated as a structural limitation.

The framework does not presuppose that decoupling is achievable. It specifies the experimental conditions under which the conjecture would be refuted.

\boxed{\text{Never optimize a single criterion.}}

The objective is to determine whether the Pareto front between cleansing efficacy and biological perturbation can be shifted by formulation design. If it can, SSC becomes a category. If it cannot, it becomes a well-characterized negative result.

